This is a training simulator. The study GLV-12-301, the drug Glyvantra (GLV-12), the sponsor
Glyvant Pharmaceuticals, the sites, the subjects and all data are fictional. Any resemblance
to real studies, products, organisations or people is coincidental. The app is not a
validated system and must not hold real patient data. Everything runs in the browser; nothing
is sent to a server.

CDISC SDTM, SDTMIG, CDASH and Controlled Terminology are standards published by CDISC
(cdisc.org). Names, domain codes, variable names and codelist values are used as CDISC
publishes them, for education. The SDTMIG v4.0 mode follows a public-review draft and is not
the final standard. MedDRA and WHODrug are not included; coded values in the data are
fictional look-alikes.

The studies PKX-07-101 (drug PKX-07, sponsor Altamira Therapeutics) and ONK-44-302 (drug
ONK-44, sponsor Meridale Oncology), their laboratory and imaging vendors and sites are fictional
too. Docetaxel is named as the comparator only. AIR-09-202 (drug AIR-09, sponsor Northwind
Respiratory) and ALV-3-301 (Aurelivax, sponsor Aurelis Vaccines), with their spirometry,
e-diary and serology vendors and sites, are fictional as well; the trainer's ALV-3-301 participants are not the CDM
Console's. ZEN-T-0428 (ZEN-55, Zenara Biosciences), INS-22-305 (Basalqin, Corvane Metabolic),
CRD-31-401 (Cardelvo, Halvern Cardiovascular) and PNR-18-203 (Dorsavin, Meadowlane Pharma),
with their test facility, core laboratory, adjudication committee, diary vendors and sites, are
fictional too. Insulin glargine and paracetamol are named as the comparator and the rescue
medicine only.

